WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.482
Cargo is more than the command Rust
developers use to build code.

2
00:00:03.562 --> 00:00:06.600
It defines packages, resolves
dependencies, enables features,

3
00:00:06.680 --> 00:00:10.049
runs build-time logic, manages lockfiles,
interacts with registries,

4
00:00:10.129 --> 00:00:11.841
and supports publishing workflows.

5
00:00:11.921 --> 00:00:15.897
Those responsibilities put Cargo directly
inside the software supply chain for Rust

6
00:00:15.977 --> 00:00:18.904
applications, libraries, command-line
tools, and services.

7
00:00:18.984 --> 00:00:21.414
Rust gives teams strong memory-safety
properties,

8
00:00:21.494 --> 00:00:24.421
but memory safety does not remove
dependency and release risk.

9
00:00:24.501 --> 00:00:28.035
A project can be written in safe Rust and
still depend on unnecessary crates,

10
00:00:28.115 --> 00:00:31.595
surprising default features, stale
versions, unreviewed build scripts,

11
00:00:31.675 --> 00:00:35.485
leaked registry credentials, or release
artifacts that nobody can trace back to a

12
00:00:35.565 --> 00:00:36.670
reviewed source state.

13
00:00:36.750 --> 00:00:40.450
The important security idea is that
everything Cargo builds becomes part of the

14
00:00:40.530 --> 00:00:41.359
trusted product.

15
00:00:41.439 --> 00:00:43.758
Direct dependencies, transitive
dependencies,

16
00:00:43.838 --> 00:00:47.096
feature-selected code paths, procedural
macros, native libraries,

17
00:00:47.176 --> 00:00:50.987
generated code, and package metadata can
all influence what reaches production.

18
00:00:51.067 --> 00:00:54.105
A secure team knows what those inputs are
and why they are present.

19
00:00:54.185 --> 00:00:57.885
Cargo security fundamentals are not about
distrusting every crate or freezing

20
00:00:57.965 --> 00:00:58.627
development.

21
00:00:58.707 --> 00:01:01.635
They are about making dependency and build
decisions visible.

22
00:01:01.715 --> 00:01:05.304
Teams need practical habits for reviewing
manifests, lockfiles, features,

23
00:01:05.384 --> 00:01:08.864
build-time code, registries, credentials,
advisory signals, licenses,

24
00:01:08.944 --> 00:01:10.766
CI/CD behavior, and release evidence.

25
00:01:10.846 --> 00:01:14.160
The defensive goal is a repeatable build
process with governed inputs.

26
00:01:14.240 --> 00:01:17.222
A reviewer should be able to understand
what code is being built,

27
00:01:17.302 --> 00:01:19.456
where it came from, what features are
enabled,

28
00:01:19.536 --> 00:01:22.352
which tools produced the artifact, who can
publish changes,

29
00:01:22.432 --> 00:01:24.641
and what evidence supports release
readiness.

30
00:01:24.721 --> 00:01:26.820
That visibility is the foundation for
trust.
